ltures and discuss  current technologies  used for rapid identification then i  will outline the experience with  root card septic typer that we have had  lastly we will review  a couple of patient cases where sepsi  typer was used to identify the isolate  in a blood  culture and the impact it had on patient  care  copynet is a regional laboratory that  services five hospitals  two standalone emergency rooms and  hundreds of doctors offices  microbiology testing for hospitals is  consolidated into one location  our laboratory has a full service  microbiology lab  that includes all of the major  subspecialty areas such as bacteriology  parasitology psychology aop testing  serology  and molecular testing we have 46  employees in our microbiology department  that work on all three shifts  in addition to 11 employees that work in  serology and the molecular area  blood cultures are drawn at each  hospital site and  are sent to the microbiology lab for  incubation and testing  we do about 5 000 blood cultures per  month  before talking about testing let's do a  review of what sepsis is and its  implication for the patient sepsis is  the body's immune response to an  infection this is caused by a  dysregulated  immune response that can be extreme and  affect multiple systems in the body  in many cases this is a life-threatening  illness according to the cdc  about 1.7 million americans develop  cessus  each year and about 270 000 of them die  about one out of every three patients in  the hospital have sepsis  and is it is the 10th most common cause  of death in the u.s  sepsis can be divided into three  categories depending on the severity  in sepsis the patient will have fever  increased heart rate  rapid breathing and other signs of  infections these patients have a  an 18 mortality rate in severe sepsis  all of the symptoms of sepsis may be  present but the patient will also have  organ dysfunction  and the mortality rate increases to 40  percent  the most severe form 