w diagnostic  tests that allow for the rapid  identification  of blood culture isolates so why is this  important when the patient has sepsis  studies have shown that the sooner they  are placed on  the appropriate antibiotic the better  their outcome will be for this reason  patients are that are suspected of  having sepsis are placed on broad  spectrum antibiotics to treat  the causative organism prior to the  blood culture becoming positive  the ability to use rapid identification  methods to get the patient  on the best antibiotic has a number of  advantages there is less exposure to  unneeded antibiotics  which in turn reduces the risk of  resistance development  and other side effects the patients also  have become  have better outcomes with reduced health  care costs  since the broad spectrum antibiotics are  used empirically some patients are  placed on antibiotics that are not  effective against the cause of agent  mortality increases each hour the  patient stays on the wrong antibiotic  rapid diagnostic tests help shorten the  time required to get them on the correct  antibiotic  there are a number of tests on the  market for rapid identification of blood  culture isolates  let's discuss each of these  the first test is the pna fish test by  option  advantix this assay utilizes dna probes  that are tagged with fluorescent  chromophores  a gram stain is performed to determine  which reagent kit to use  and blood from the positive bottle is  placed on the slide the reagent is then  added to the slide and the dna probe  is allowed to bind with the nucleic acid  of the organism in the sample  the reaction takes 20 minutes and the  slide is read by using the fluorescent  microscope  the color of the organism on the slide  is based upon the dna probe  which attached to the organism and  determines the identification  for example in the gram-negative bacilli  kit  there are three dna probes if e coli is  present the organism will stain green  klebsiella pneumonia will stain yellow  while pseudomonas originosa  stains red there are kits for four  separate organism groups including  staphylococci  from negative isoli enterococcus and  candida  biofire manufactures the film array  blood culture identification kit  this is a multiplex pcr test that allows  identification of 26 bacterial targets  and 7 yeast targets  in addition 10 resistance genes are  detected  along with the organism identification  all organisms and resistance genes are  detected using a single cartridge  and this assay an aliquot of the blood  is injected into the cartridge  that is shown in the lower left corner  of this slide this cartridge contains  all the necessary  agents to perform the multiplex pcr the  cartridge is placed in  the instrument and results are available  in about an hour  luminex varigene also produces a  multiplex pcr test  they differ from the biofire and that  there is a separate panel for  gram-positive and gram-negative  organisms  the gram-positive panel detects 13  bacterial targets as well as three  resistance genes  the gram-negative panel detects nine  bacterial targets with six resistance  genes  like the bio-fire assay the blood is  injected into the cartridge and placed  into the instruments  results are available in about two hours  genmark is another company that makes  multiplex pcr assays for blood culture  identification  they have three separate cartridges the  gram positive panel detects 20  bacterial targets and two resistance  genes in addition the  the cartridge contains probes for pan  gram negative  and pan candidate targets allowing you  to to detect mixed infections  or it directs you to the use of another  panel  when the grain stain is questionable the  gram negative panel  detects 21 targets with six resistance  genes it also contains probes for pan  grand positive and pan candidate targets  the third panel is a yeast panel that  contains 15 targets just like the other  multiplex assays  an aliquot blood is taken from the  bottle and injected into the cartridge  once placed in the e-plex instrument the  test takes about an hour and a half  the last rapid identification method we  will discuss and the main focus of my  talk today  is the broker mbt sexy typer this method  utilizes the multi-toff technology and  has the potential to identify any  organism that is contained in the  broker multitap database identifications  may be batched or performed individually  when performing a cost analysis of  different rapid identification methods  the sepsi typer was least expensive for  us  before we talk more about the sepsi  typer let's review the theory behind the  multitop method  multitof is an acronym for matrix  assisted laser desorption ionization  time of flight  it uses mass spectrometry that was  initially used in microbiology to  identify organisms growing on a culture  plate  to perform the identification from a  culture plate the organism  is inoculated to the target plate as  shown here in the direct method the  organisms  is placed directly onto the plate  depending on the organism it may be  necessary to use the extended direct  method  which uses formic acid to break down the  cell wall prior to inoculating the  target  organisms with a thick cell wall may  require an extraction prior to  inoculating the plate  once the organism dries on the target  plate a matrix is added which absorbs  the irradiation of the  laser and helps the ionization of the  particles the target plate is then  placed into the instrument  once a target plate is in the instrument  a laser is emitted  that will strike the organism well on  the plate  this causes two things to occur the  organism is vaporized  and the particles that are created are  ionized giving them a positive  charge  these charged particles are then  accelerated toward  a detector plate by the the potential  difference between the target and the  electrodes  the mass and charge of the particles  determine how long it 